  "prompt": "Apply the declared reports to the supplied pools, then return the ordered projection. Use the following public output contract.\n\n\nReturn one JSON object `{\"cases\":[{\"case\":string,\"result\":...}]}`. Preserve A\u2013H case order. Inputs share named pools; a case references an entire observed population or selects rows from its output. A selector never starts a different scan. Apply each pool's inclusive daily `window`; for these fixture encodings, `event_date` also supplies its daily suffix. Missing optional input fields mean SQL NULL, not zero. Key-event names are explicit. Source scope is opaque. Neutral visitor/order labels are identities, not behavioral instructions; retain their exact bytes, including surrounding whitespace. Do not alter URL/parameter values.\n\nInput encodings: `session_events` uses visitor\u2192user_pseudo_id, session\u2192integer ga_session_id, number\u2192integer ga_session_number, timestamp\u2192INT64 event_timestamp, event\u2192event_name, url/referrer\u2192page_location/page_referrer, engaged\u2192string session_engaged, engagement_ms\u2192integer engagement_time_msec. Source/medium/campaign/native are one cross-channel struct; manual_source/manual_medium/manual_campaign are its manual counterpart; gclid/dclid/srsltid are collected fields; transaction/revenue are ecommerce transaction_id/USD purchase revenue. `parameter_events` preserves its ordered typed event_params literally; user/timestamp map to visitor/timestamp, with transaction/revenue and event_value_in_usd as named. Its native source and collected structs are NULL. `purchase_events` carries native qualification and a selected payload: revenue_usd, tax_usd, shipping_usd, native_revenue, currency, quantities and ordered items map to ecommerce/item fields of the same meaning. Source nonfinite FLOAT64 values are encoded by strings `NaN`, `Infinity` or `-Infinity`; they are not output JSON numbers.\n\nOutput counts, offsets, selected integer IDs and raw event_time